/* The section head: a plain, light heading — the same voice as a card title, one step larger.
 *
 * It was uppercase, tracked and grey, and a column of those read as shouted grey labels rather
 * than headings (owner, 2026-08-07). The rank now comes from size and colour alone: caps and
 * letter-spacing were doing the work that a heading does by being a heading, and they made a
 * long label ("THE SQUAD · 2 PEOPLE") harder to read than the content under it. */
.section-label {
  font-family: var(--font-family-display);
  font-size: var(--font-lg);
  font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  line-height: var(--leading-tight);
  color: var(--foreground);
}

/* The label owns a small gap to the thing it introduces (reported 2026-08-20:
 * headings sat glued to their cards wherever the wrapper was a plain <section>).
 * A sibling margin rather than per-screen stacks, so the rhythm is the system's
 * decision; where a flex parent already spaces children the margin adds a step,
 * which is the same visual statement — the label belongs to what follows. */
.section-label + * {
  margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
}

/* INSIDE A CARD THE CARD OWNS THE RHYTHM. Card.css states that once, for every
 * guest; this margin was added on top of the card's own gap and the two summed
 * to 20px — five grid units, a step the scale does not have (found on a product's
 * team screen, 2026-08-26, by the audit rule that had been quietly rejecting
 * real steps and so hid this one). The card's gap already separates its
 * children, and where a component needs the extra step, Card.css gives it. */
.card > .section-label + * {
  margin-block-start: 0;
}
